  "notes": "An 80s rapper turned 2000s R&B/hip-hop producer; the research windows the entry **2000–2010 as a producer** because his own 1987–1995 performing run fails Gate 2, and that window is used verbatim. The band is \"84–100 straight\", marked `[LOW-CONF]`, so it is used as written with the mode at 92. The drum deltas come from his documented rig, quoted in the entry: \"drum machine plus live instrumentation — 'I want to be on stage with my drum machine and live instrumentation. The way I make a record, I want to bring that to the stage'\". So a programmed kick/snare grid sits under a four-lane conga/tambourine/shaker/ride bed at three to six hits a bar, with `quantizeStrength` at 0.68 and `velocityVar` at 0.24 — the played layer — and a swung 0.57 16th hat at 0.38 fill density; the snare carries real ghost activity 2–6 ms off the grid. His stated method drives the melodic side: \"he makes the track and then writes the hook himself — 'I made the track and wrote the hook. I had to sing the hook and convince her'\", and he refuses to send generic beats, working only in-room. So the melody is authored as a real sung hook (a 0.82 chord-tone bias over a mid-tenor register) rather than as a chant, and the counter answers it. ⚠ Confidence is **low** and the research says why: \"method quotes and credits sourced; tempo/harmony parameters are inferred and marked\". The key claim is `[LOW-CONF]` too, so the keys here are lane-level rather than named, and every velocity, density and register number is INFERRED.",
